{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias beyond ~1 week: the model systematically under-forecasts during sustained uptrends (true path runs ~+1 to +4 EUR above forecast from ~day 7–25 in most examples). Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger from days 7–20).",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts follow the direction but with insufficient slope (lagging rallies). When the last 5–10 days of history show a clear upswing, steepen the forecast trajectory (add positive drift) rather than reverting toward the recent mean.",
    "Underestimation of post-breakout persistence: after a jump to the low-70s, the model expects quick mean reversion, but realized prices stay elevated for 1–3 weeks. Reduce mean-reversion strength after upside breakouts; keep prices higher for longer.",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip near the end: at longer horizons (~day 25–30) the model often stays too high when the realized series starts to roll over (late-period drop toward mid/upper-60s). Introduce stronger late-horizon mean reversion / downside adjustment when recent history shows weakening momentum.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s amplitude is too small in strong moves (misses peaks around 72–73). Inflate forecast variance (or widen bands) and allow higher highs during bullish regimes; conversely, allow sharper late pullbacks when momentum turns."
  ]
}
